  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: The upper level ridge situated over the \nfour corners continue to bring heat in most of Northern and Central \nNevada today as temperatures climb into the upper 90s and triple \ndigits. Various heat related products are still in effect until 11 \nPM PDT this evening across all zones except for the Excessive Heat \nWarning for Southeastern Elko County ending at 11 PM PDT Monday \nevening as overnight lows remain in the upper 70s, combined with \nhigh temperatures around 100 degrees.\n\nThe upper ridge pattern is bringing in moisture over Arizona and\nthe Baja that is moving northward into Nevada. Active monsoon \nweather pattern returns across much of Central and Eastern Nevada \nthis weekend as PWAT values increase up to 0.8 to 1 inch, along with \nCAPE values increasing above 200 J/kg. A mix of wet/dry \nthunderstorms are expected across much of Central Nevada this \nafternoon, with dry thunderstorm reaching into Eastern Nevada. Storm \nmotion is expected to be a little fast early in the day, but by the \nafternoon, the motion will slow down, allowing the potential for \nmore rain reaching the valleys. Throughout the next week, the active \nmonsoon pattern is expected to continue each afternoon with a mix of \nwet/dry thunderstorms across Central and Eastern Nevada. As for \nHumboldt County, PWAT values increase up to one inch, however little \nto no instability is leading to little to no chance of active \nweather throughout the week. \n\nElevated fire weather conditions today across Northern Nevada along \nthe state border as winds expected to gust up to 20-25 mph with low \nhumidity around 10% and very high temperatures. Over the week, fire \nweather conditions will begin to ease as the monsoonal flow will \ncontinue to bring moisture, causing minimum relative humidity to be \n20% or above across the region by the end of the week. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High forecast confidence regarding \na moderate to major risk for heat-related illnesses for most of \nNorthern and Central Nevada this afternoon. High forecast confidence \nin the possibility of a few strong thunderstorms in parts of \nNorthern and Central Nevada this afternoon into this evening. \nModerate forecast confidence regarding elevated fire weather \nconditions due to hot, dry, and breezy conditions in Northern Nevada \ntoday. Moderate forecast confidence in a cooling trend Monday \nthrough Friday. No deviations from the NBM model output.",
